looks like for how you can use
Google Ads and Google Cloud

00:25:42.170 --> 00:25:44.480
products together
to solve for the use

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.380
case of purchase prediction.

00:25:50.060 --> 00:25:53.050
Now at this point, you may
be thinking, OK, I get it.

00:25:53.050 --> 00:25:57.250
Customers are asking for
personalized, customized

00:25:57.250 --> 00:26:01.000
experiences and this
has real business impact

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:04.060
if we are able to solve this
problem of breaking down

00:26:04.060 --> 00:26:06.960
these data silos, bringing
data together, and analyzing it

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.870
at scale.

00:26:08.870 --> 00:26:11.270
But I live in a multi cloud
environment and why should

00:26:11.270 --> 00:26:12.680
I do this with Google?

00:26:12.680 --> 00:26:15.020
So I want to just walk
through a couple of areas

00:26:15.020 --> 00:26:16.670
where we're different
specifically

00:26:16.670 --> 00:26:19.150
for Cloud for Marketing.

00:26:19.150 --> 00:26:23.230
First, we're a leader in
smart analytics as a service.

00:26:23.230 --> 00:26:25.830
Forester rated Google
as the only leader

00:26:25.830 --> 00:26:30.530
in their insight platforms
as a service report.

00:26:30.530 --> 00:26:33.620
We're also building solutions
that are specifically designed

00:26:33.620 --> 00:26:37.160
to meet marketers' needs.

00:26:37.160 --> 00:26:41.410
So an example are the use cases
that I just walked through,

00:26:41.410 --> 00:26:44.950
as well as making it easier
to use these solutions

00:26:44.950 --> 00:26:47.560
themselves through tools like
the BigQuery data transfer

00:26:47.560 --> 00:26:52.240
service, as well as things
like allowing a marketer

00:26:52.240 --> 00:26:57.790
to build machine learning
models using BigQuery ML

00:26:57.790 --> 00:26:59.770
and doing that right in SQL.

00:26:59.770 --> 00:27:02.860
And while we're on the
topic of ML and AI,

00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:04.960
Google is a leader in this area.

00:27:04.960 --> 00:27:09.760
We have over 7,500 projects
that are powered by AI inside

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:13.060
of Google, including some of
Google's consumer products,

00:27:13.060 --> 00:27:17.430
like Search, Maps,
Assistant, and more.

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:21.120
And we've embedded ML and AI
into the core of our solutions

00:27:21.120 --> 00:27:23.610
in Google Cloud to
make it accessible

00:27:23.610 --> 00:27:26.190
and enable those
transformative experiences.

00:27:30.200 --> 00:27:33.615
So in addition to the Cloud
for Marketing specific ways

00:27:33.615 --> 00:27:35.740
that we're different, we
also build on all the ways

00:27:35.740 --> 00:27:37.323
that Google Cloud
itself is different.

00:27:37.323 --> 00:27:38.740
And you've heard
a lot about this

00:27:38.740 --> 00:27:40.810
over the course of the
week in our keynotes,

00:27:40.810 --> 00:27:43.370
so I won't go into
it in great detail.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:46.150
But just to highlight a
few of our strengths here.

00:27:46.150 --> 00:27:47.740
Security.

00:27:47.740 --> 00:27:51.960
Fully automated operations
and openness are in our DNA.

00:27:54.800 --> 00:27:58.100
And lastly, we bring
in the best of Google

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:00.050
to solving marketing use cases.

00:28:02.780 --> 00:28:06.680
Things like using the technology
of the Google Cloud Vision API

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:09.500
to be able to solve
for unstructured data

00:28:09.500 --> 00:28:13.340
and getting value out of that
visual unstructured data.

00:28:13.340 --> 00:28:16.460
Plus Google teams have
years of experience

00:28:16.460 --> 00:28:19.070
in working with
enterprises of all kinds

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:22.760
across the globe to solve
marketing challenges using

00:28:22.760 --> 00:28:24.980
Cloud and Ads technology.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:27.680
This means that Google can
help you really accelerate

00:28:27.680 --> 00:28:29.534
your digital transformation.

00:28:32.260 --> 00:28:35.850
So we've talked a lot about how
we're helping enterprises today

00:28:35.850 --> 00:28:38.650
by leveraging our
products better together.

00:28:38.650 --> 00:28:41.790
And I want to just give you a
sense for what we're building.

00:28:41.790 --> 00:28:46.140
How we're turning that linear
data journey into a flywheel

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:49.690
that can keep pace with customer
expectations and decision

00:28:49.690 --> 00:28:50.190
making.

00:28:53.380 --> 00:28:57.300
The Cloud for Marketing
console is our first step

00:28:57.300 --> 00:28:59.370
in making it possible
for marketers

00:28:59.370 --> 00:29:01.830
to solve for use
cases in a way that's

00:29:01.830 --> 00:29:06.480
faster, simpler, and smarter,
and it's available in alpha

00:29:06.480 --> 00:29:07.770
now.

00:29:07.770 --> 00:29:10.380
If you're interested in
seeing more about the console

00:29:10.380 --> 00:29:14.950
and asking questions in detail,
you can do that at our booth.

00:29:14.950 --> 00:29:17.550
And there's product managers
and engineers on site

00:29:17.550 --> 00:29:20.940
to help answer your questions.

00:29:20.940 --> 00:29:23.110
Within the existing
Cloud console,

00:29:23.110 --> 00:29:25.830
we've created this Cloud
for Marketing console

00:29:25.830 --> 00:29:28.800
to make it more
engaging and interactive

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.500
for non-technical users.

00:29:31.500 --> 00:29:33.930
When a user opens
the console, they'll

00:29:33.930 --> 00:29:38.250
see a dashboard that will have
available data sources based

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:41.130
on the data they've
brought into BigQuery,

00:29:41.130 --> 00:29:45.470
as well as built in tagging
and access controls.

00:29:45.470 --> 00:29:47.870
We have templated queries
that make it easier

00:29:47.870 --> 00:29:51.620
for you to do to get value
out of your data and derive

00:29:51.620 --> 00:29:53.150
insights.

00:29:53.150 --> 00:29:54.830
And predictive
models that help you

00:29:54.830 --> 00:29:58.890
understand and predict customer
behavior and drive business

00:29:58.890 --> 00:29:59.390
outcomes.

00:30:02.660 --> 00:30:07.450
And finally, we have links
to the Data Studio dashboards

00:30:07.450 --> 00:30:11.620
that Matt described
that are built directly

00:30:11.620 --> 00:30:13.520
into the console as a template.

00:30:17.010 --> 00:30:18.990
We make bringing your
data into BigQuery

00:30:18.990 --> 00:30:21.390
faster by providing
tools that give you

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:24.275
the step by step instructions
you need directly

00:30:24.275 --> 00:30:24.900
in the console.

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:29.920
And managing all of your
marketing-related data

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:34.870
is simpler with built in
tagging and access controls.

00:30:34.870 --> 00:30:38.110
You can also maintain data
privacy and security down

00:30:38.110 --> 00:30:44.070
to the column level for
sensitive data and PII.

00:30:44.070 --> 00:30:47.100
And finally, the
console provides smarter

00:30:47.100 --> 00:30:51.930
insights about your marketing
customers and products

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:55.560
by leveraging templated
queries and the same models

00:30:55.560 --> 00:30:59.160
that power Google's services
with no coding needed.

00:31:01.690 --> 00:31:04.060
Now as we build products
like the console

00:31:04.060 --> 00:31:08.170
to help make it possible to
solve for marketing use cases

00:31:08.170 --> 00:31:10.810
faster, simpler,
and smarter, we're

00:31:10.810 --> 00:31:14.260
also trying to make it
easier for enterprises

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:19.690
to get the most value of how our
products work better together.

00:31:19.690 --> 00:31:24.430
As an example of this, we
include templated queries

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:27.070
for queries that you
might need to run

00:31:27.070 --> 00:31:30.190
around Google Analytics
or other web analytics

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:33.150
data in the console.

00:31:33.150 --> 00:31:35.380
And we also have
seamless integrations

00:31:35.380 --> 00:31:38.500
into Data Studio built in.

00:31:38.500 --> 00:31:41.070
We're excited to see
what kinds of experiences

00:31:41.070 --> 00:31:45.840
you all build by using Google
Cloud and Google Ads products

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:47.778
effectively better together.

00:31:47.778 --> 00:31:50.070
And I want to give you a
little bit of inspiration here

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:53.385
and bring up one of our
customers, Kenny Wu, who

00:31:53.385 --> 00:31:55.980
is Vise President of
product at Essence,

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:57.300
to tell you how he's doing it.

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:00.216
[APPLAUSE]

00:32:00.216 --> 00:32:03.140
KENNY WU: Thank you.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:06.590
Hello, I'm Kenny.

00:32:06.590 --> 00:32:10.400
I look after the
product team at Essence.

00:32:10.400 --> 00:32:13.580
And today I'd like to talk
to you a bit about how we've

00:32:13.580 --> 00:32:16.577
used machine learning
in Google Cloud

00:32:16.577 --> 00:32:18.410
and activated that to
create better customer

00:32:18.410 --> 00:32:23.540
experiences via the
Google Marketing Platform.

00:32:23.540 --> 00:32:27.200
Essence is an advertising
agency, part of GroupM and WPP.

00:32:27.200 --> 00:32:30.260
And we're focused on
creating better experiences

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:32.947
and making better
decisions using data.

00:32:32.947 --> 00:32:34.780
We work with some of
the largest advertisers

00:32:34.780 --> 00:32:40.690
in the world, Target, NBC
Universal, T-Mobile, and Google

00:32:40.690 --> 00:32:42.700
to name a few.

00:32:42.700 --> 00:32:47.020
Today I'll be taking you through
an example of how we customized

00:32:47.020 --> 00:32:50.500
ad creative with Google, but we
do this for many of our clients

00:32:50.500 --> 00:32:53.670
across many industries.

00:32:53.670 --> 00:32:56.230
So first.

00:32:56.230 --> 00:32:59.590
The world right now
is buzzing with talks

00:32:59.590 --> 00:33:02.330
of artificial intelligence
and machine learning.

00:33:02.330 --> 00:33:05.480
The science of making
things smart and the science

00:33:05.480 --> 00:33:07.850
of getting computers to
do something without being

00:33:07.850 --> 00:33:10.423
programmed with the rules.

00:33:10.423 --> 00:33:12.340
While there's a lot of
theory that's going on.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:14.798
I'd like to share a concrete
example of how we brought this

00:33:14.798 --> 00:33:16.970
to life.

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:21.350
So a few months ago,
Google Marketing

00:33:21.350 --> 00:33:23.740
came to us with a challenge.

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:26.040
How do we help consumers
better understand

00:33:26.040 --> 00:33:27.960
the power of Google Assistant?

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:30.600
Specifically this
idea of how a system

00:33:30.600 --> 00:33:36.310
can offer helpful information at
the moment when it's relevant.

00:33:36.310 --> 00:33:39.800
So a good example of
this is in the kitchen.

00:33:39.800 --> 00:33:41.140
Any home cooks out there?

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:43.160
People that are
interested, yeah.

00:33:43.160 --> 00:33:47.660
So I was making pancakes the
other day and for those science

00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:49.820
geeks out there we
know that you leaven

00:33:49.820 --> 00:33:52.760
this dough with a bit of
soda and a bit of acid.

00:33:52.760 --> 00:33:55.992
And I was replacing
vinegar with lemon juice.

00:33:55.992 --> 00:33:57.450
But I had my hands
covered in flour

00:33:57.450 --> 00:34:01.160
and forgot what
the conversion was.

00:34:01.160 --> 00:34:06.470
Great moment to find some
information via Assistant.

00:34:06.470 --> 00:34:10.909
So what we did is we took
this idea of helping people

00:34:10.909 --> 00:34:15.530
in the right moments find the
information that they need,

00:34:15.530 --> 00:34:18.860
specifically around a recipe
section in a partnership

00:34:18.860 --> 00:34:21.610
with The Guardian.

00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:24.730
What we looked at
specifically was demonstrating

00:34:24.730 --> 00:34:28.090
how Google Assistant could
recommend relevant recipes

00:34:28.090 --> 00:34:31.250
based on the recipes
that you're looking at.

00:34:31.250 --> 00:34:33.250
So this idea of
contextual advertising

00:34:33.250 --> 00:34:35.630
has existed for a long time.

00:34:35.630 --> 00:34:38.199
But usually, you actually don't
have that much interesting

00:34:38.199 --> 00:34:38.980
information.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:41.560
You know a web page where
something is served,

00:34:41.560 --> 00:34:43.600
roughly what the topic
looks like, potentially

00:34:43.600 --> 00:34:46.489
where the user is located.

00:34:46.489 --> 00:34:50.199
And if you're lucky, you get
keyword-level information.

00:34:50.199 --> 00:34:52.770
So something we
might have considered

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:56.909
with just that information is
to do some text categorization.

00:34:56.909 --> 00:34:59.640
Let's categorize sweet keywords
and savory ones and then

00:34:59.640 --> 00:35:02.220
recommend an
accompanied recipe that

00:35:02.220 --> 00:35:07.320
would be relevant to something
sweet or something savory.

00:35:07.320 --> 00:35:10.190
However, you could end up
with some weird results.

00:35:10.190 --> 00:35:12.770
Pancakes, which for me is
a sweet breakfast food,

00:35:12.770 --> 00:35:15.323
can also mean kimchi pancakes.

00:35:15.323 --> 00:35:16.740
And a recommendation
for something

00:35:16.740 --> 00:35:21.460
like orange-infused maple syrup
would not be very appropriate.

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:23.210
So we took a look at
the other information

00:35:23.210 --> 00:35:27.950
that we had at hand,
which was the pictures

00:35:27.950 --> 00:35:30.488
of the recipes themselves.

00:35:30.488 --> 00:35:32.530
And took a look at how we
could take this content

00:35:32.530 --> 00:35:34.680
to customize our messaging.

00:35:34.680 --> 00:35:40.950
We used AutoML in Cloud,
and specifically Vision,

00:35:40.950 --> 00:35:46.020
to train a model on identifying
sweet and savory foods using

00:35:46.020 --> 00:35:47.130
the imagery alone.

00:35:47.130 --> 00:35:48.880
So without text analysis.

00:35:48.880 --> 00:35:49.960
Took a little time.

00:35:49.960 --> 00:35:51.900
About 200 images
that needs to be hand

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:54.280
categorized into
sweet and savory.

00:35:54.280 --> 00:35:57.390
But afterwards,
we're very accurately

00:35:57.390 --> 00:36:00.760
able to predict whether or not
something was sweet or savory.

00:36:00.760 --> 00:36:04.080
And that allowed us to come
up with much more contextually

00:36:04.080 --> 00:36:07.835
relevant messaging, without the
weirdness of kimchi pancakes

00:36:07.835 --> 00:36:08.460
in maple syrup.

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:15.120
So putting this all together
with text analysis and image

00:36:15.120 --> 00:36:17.850
analysis, we're
able to create ads

00:36:17.850 --> 00:36:21.360
that appeared customized
and conversational with just

00:36:21.360 --> 00:36:23.390
content and context.

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:25.310
This can then be
extended in the future

00:36:25.310 --> 00:36:27.830
by bringing in the first
party and third party

00:36:27.830 --> 00:36:34.460
data that was mentioned earlier
to create even better ads.

00:36:34.460 --> 00:36:37.160
While this feels
great having ads

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:40.220
that make sense, that are
relevant to the content that's

00:36:40.220 --> 00:36:44.020
next to you, it has big
business impact as well.

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.600
We're able to create
thousands of ads

00:36:46.600 --> 00:36:49.990
in a matter of hours across
each individual recipe page.

00:36:49.990 --> 00:36:52.930
We also applied similar
methodology on things

00:36:52.930 --> 00:36:58.480
like movie reviews, restaurant
reviews, travel to name a few.

00:36:58.480 --> 00:37:03.480
And we saw a major
lift in response

00:37:03.480 --> 00:37:05.760
based on more relevant ads.

00:37:05.760 --> 00:37:10.860
In fact, we saw a 9.5% lift
in category understanding.

00:37:10.860 --> 00:37:12.360
And this is interesting
because that

00:37:12.360 --> 00:37:17.810
means that one in 10 people who
saw these ads versus a control

00:37:17.810 --> 00:37:21.530
had a much better understanding
of what Google Assistant could

00:37:21.530 --> 00:37:25.710
do for them on a
day by day basis.

00:37:25.710 --> 00:37:26.900
So how did we do this?

00:37:26.900 --> 00:37:28.400
So here's the
technical architecture

00:37:28.400 --> 00:37:30.890
behind all of this.

00:37:30.890 --> 00:37:32.750
What we did is on
a twice daily basis

00:37:32.750 --> 00:37:34.520
collect information
from The Guardian

00:37:34.520 --> 00:37:38.120
in a JSON file about all the
news articles, recipe articles,

00:37:38.120 --> 00:37:40.700
reviews, et cetera
that were published.

00:37:40.700 --> 00:37:46.380
And we analyzed that
information in Google Cloud.

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:49.020
First off, we took a
look at the content.

00:37:49.020 --> 00:37:50.110
Every brand is different.

00:37:50.110 --> 00:37:55.020
So some brands want to
appear next to a large swath

00:37:55.020 --> 00:37:56.730
of content, some very narrow.

00:37:56.730 --> 00:38:03.450
So we actually trained an AutoML
natural language processing

00:38:03.450 --> 00:38:07.830
model to determine whether or
not the content was appropriate

00:38:07.830 --> 00:38:09.960
for that brand.

00:38:09.960 --> 00:38:13.050
Second, we used the
Natural Language

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:18.660
API to extract salient keywords,
such as in the last example,

00:38:18.660 --> 00:38:23.480
I think ginger, here
a salient keyword,

00:38:23.480 --> 00:38:26.210
from the text in the recipe.

00:38:26.210 --> 00:38:28.220
Further, we used
the visual analysis,

00:38:28.220 --> 00:38:32.180
as I mentioned before, to
determine sweet and savory.

00:38:32.180 --> 00:38:34.400
Finally, we took a
look at additional data

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:36.830
we could use to bring in
and enrich the information

00:38:36.830 --> 00:38:38.510
that we had already.

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:40.370
A good example of
this is Maps data

00:38:40.370 --> 00:38:43.730
that we used around
restaurant reviews.

00:38:43.730 --> 00:38:47.300
Given that The Guardian is
focused very much in the UK,

00:38:47.300 --> 00:38:51.050
we looked at where a person's
location was and whether or not

00:38:51.050 --> 00:38:52.730
the restaurant was
within the city.

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:55.610
At which point we would
recommend a restaurant

00:38:55.610 --> 00:38:56.900
in a particular neighborhood.

00:38:56.900 --> 00:38:59.210
Or if it's outside of
the city, and we would

00:38:59.210 --> 00:39:01.780
indicate which town it was in.

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:03.340
All this combined
together allowed

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:06.190
us to construct a
customized copy that

00:39:06.190 --> 00:39:10.130
was then fed and activated in
the Google Marking Platform.

00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:13.060
So the two pieces of the
Marking Platform that we used,

00:39:13.060 --> 00:39:15.640
Studio, where we
created the dynamic ads,

00:39:15.640 --> 00:39:20.080
combined the customized
text with the ad imagery.

00:39:20.080 --> 00:39:24.280
And then we serve those ads via
Google Marketing Platform's DV

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:26.500
360.

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:29.080
With a special connection
with The Guardian,

00:39:29.080 --> 00:39:32.620
we're able to determine which
pages would be served as we're

00:39:32.620 --> 00:39:36.610
buying impressions
through DV 360,

00:39:36.610 --> 00:39:40.600
so therefore we could customize
the content that would show up.

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:44.100
So I hope that this
short example inspires

00:39:44.100 --> 00:39:45.600
you to take a close
look at the data

00:39:45.600 --> 00:39:48.390
that you have on hand, not
just the data that you might

00:39:48.390 --> 00:39:50.880
be using today, to
create better experiences

00:39:50.880 --> 00:39:54.420
and make better decisions for
your customers, your clients,

00:39:54.420 --> 00:39:56.678
and your team

00:39:56.678 --> 00:40:00.534
[APPLAUSE]

00:40:00.534 --> 00:40:01.948
Thanks.

00:40:01.948 --> 00:40:03.740
MICHELLE KRAEMER: Thank
you so much, Kenny.

00:40:03.740 --> 00:40:07.042
You've managed to figure out how
to make this entire room even

00:40:07.042 --> 00:40:09.250
hungrier when we're the only
